steps.body:
A loop does not have to visit every element one by one. Two tools change that.

**Step by more than 1.** `i += 2` jumps two indexes each pass. Start at 0 for the even indexes, or at 1 for the odd ones:

```java
for (int i = 0; i < a.length; i += 2) {
    System.out.println(a[i]); // a[0], a[2], a[4], ...
}
```

**Skip with continue.** `continue` ends the current pass and jumps to the next one. Put the skip test first, so the rest of the body only handles the values you keep:

```java
for (int x : a) {
    if (x % 2 != 0) {
        continue; // odd, skip it
    }
    System.out.println(x);
}
```

Gotcha: test odd with `x % 2 != 0`, never `x % 2 == 1`. In Java the remainder takes the sign of the left side, so `-3 % 2` is `-1`, and `x % 2 == 1` quietly misses every negative odd number. `x % 2 == 0` for even is safe either way.

example.code:
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] nums = {4, 7, -3, 10, 15, 8, -6, 1};

        System.out.print("Even indexes:");
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i += 2) {
            System.out.print(" " + nums[i]);
        }
        System.out.println();

        System.out.print("Even values:");
        for (int x : nums) {
            if (x % 2 != 0) {
                continue; // skips 7, -3, 15, and 1
            }
            System.out.print(" " + x);
        }
        System.out.println();
    }
}

