            if (x % 3 != 0) {
                continue;
            }
            System.out.print(" " + x);
        }
        System.out.println();
    }
}

steps.body:
To walk an array backwards, start at the last index and count down to 0. Use `>= 0`, not `> 0`, or you never read `a[0]`:

```java
for (int i = a.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
    System.out.print(a[i] + " ");
}
```

The same loop builds a reversed copy: `add` each element to a new `ArrayList` as you go.

**Removing from a list.** `list.remove(i)` deletes index `i` and shifts every later element one place left. Walk backwards and that shift only touches elements you already checked. It is the index loop alternative to the `Iterator` from the last lesson:

```java
for (int i = list.size() - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
    if (list.get(i) < 0) {
        list.remove(i);
    }
}
```

Gotcha: a forward loop that removes skips the element right after each removal. Remove at index 3 and the next element slides into index 3, but `i++` moves on to 4, so two negatives in a row leave the second one behind. `i` is an `int` here, so `remove(i)` removes by index, not by value.

example.code:
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] temps = {3, -2, -5, 7, 0, 4};

        System.out.print("Backwards:");
        for (int i = temps.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            System.out.print(" " + temps[i]);
        }
        System.out.println();

        List<Integer> reversed = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int i = temps.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            reversed.add(temps[i]);
        }
        System.out.println("Reversed list: " + reversed);

        for (int i = reversed.size() - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            if (reversed.get(i) < 0) {
                reversed.remove(i);
            }
        }
        System.out.println("No negatives: " + reversed);
    }
}

