import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] floors = {1, 2, 4, 5, 9, 10, 12};

        System.out.print("Top to bottom:");
        for (int i = floors.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            System.out.print(" " + floors[i]);
        }
        System.out.println();

        List<Integer> evenFloors = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int i = floors.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            if (floors[i] % 2 == 0) {
                evenFloors.add(floors[i]);
            }
        }
        System.out.println("Even floors: " + evenFloors);

        for (int i = evenFloors.size() - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            if (evenFloors.get(i) > 8) {
                evenFloors.remove(i);
            }
        }
        System.out.println("Up to 8: " + evenFloors);
    }
}

steps.body:
Two pointers are two indexes into the same array. Here they start at opposite ends and walk toward each other. They meet in the middle, so the loop runs only about half as many times as the array is long.

```java
int left = 0;
int right = a.length - 1;
while (left < right) {
    int temp = a[left];
    a[left] = a[right];
    a[right] = temp;
    left++;
    right--;
}
```

That reverses `a` in place, with no second array. The swap needs `temp`: writing `a[left] = a[right]` first overwrites the value you still need.

The same walk fits in one `for` header. Declare both indexes, then update both with a comma:

```java
for (int i = 0, j = a.length - 1; i < j; i++, j--) {
    // compare or swap a[i] and a[j]
}
```

`left < right` stops the moment the pointers meet. With an odd length the middle element has no partner and stays put. A common bug is looping `i` over the whole array and swapping `a[i]` with `a[a.length - 1 - i]`: every pair swaps twice and the array ends up unchanged.

example.code:
import java.util.Arrays;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] scores = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};

        int left = 0;
        int right = scores.length - 1;
        while (left < right) {
            int temp = scores[left];
            scores[left] = scores[right];
            scores[right] = temp;
            left++;
            right--;
        }
        System.out.println("Reversed: " + Arrays.toString(scores));

        for (int i = 0, j = scores.length - 1; i < j; i++, j--) {
            int temp = scores[i];
            scores[i] = scores[j];
            scores[j] = temp;
        }
        System.out.println("Restored: " + Arrays.toString(scores));
    }
}

