import java.util.Arrays;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] first = {3, 8, 1, 8, 3};
        int[] second = {4, 6, 6, 5};

        boolean firstSame = true;
        int left = 0;
        int right = first.length - 1;
        while (left < right) {
            if (first[left] != first[right]) {
                firstSame = false;
                break;
            }
            left++;
            right--;
        }

        boolean secondSame = true;
        for (int i = 0, j = second.length - 1; i < j; i++, j--) {
            if (second[i] != second[j]) {
                secondSame = false;
                break;
            }
        }

        System.out.println(Arrays.toString(first) + " reads the same: " + firstSame);
        System.out.println(Arrays.toString(second) + " reads the same: " + secondSame);
    }
}

steps.body:
Many problems compare each element with the one before it. Start at `i = 1` so `a[i - 1]` always exists. Starting at 0 would read `a[-1]` and throw.

```java
for (int i = 1; i < a.length; i++) {
    int change = a[i] - a[i - 1];
}
```

There are `a.length - 1` neighbor pairs, so this loop runs one time fewer than the array has elements.

**Prefix sums.** A running total array holds, at each index, the sum of everything up to and including it. Make a new array of the same length, copy the first value, then add each element to the total before it:

```java
int[] running = new int[a.length];
running[0] = a[0];
for (int i = 1; i < a.length; i++) {
    running[i] = running[i - 1] + a[i];
}
```

Why bother: once it is built, the sum of any stretch from index `from` to index `to` is `running[to] - running[from - 1]` (or just `running[to]` when `from` is 0), with no second loop. `running[0] = a[0]` throws on an empty array, so check `a.length > 0` first when the array could be empty. Watch for overflow: a running total grows fast, so switch to `long[]` when the values are large.

example.code:
import java.util.Arrays;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] temps = {18, 21, 20, 23, 25, 22, 26};
        int[] rain = {3, 0, 5, 2, 4};

        int warmerDays = 0;
        for (int i = 1; i < temps.length; i++) {
            if (temps[i] > temps[i - 1]) {
                warmerDays++;
            }
        }
        System.out.println("Warmer than the day before: " + warmerDays);

        int[] running = new int[rain.length];
        running[0] = rain[0];
        for (int i = 1; i < rain.length; i++) {
            running[i] = running[i - 1] + rain[i];
        }
        System.out.println("Running rain: " + Arrays.toString(running));
        System.out.println("Days 2 to 4: " + (running[3] - running[0]));
    }
}

